{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"HANDOFF — design phase COMPLETE for the model-class work; implementation is next.\n\nSTACK: all 12 changes are LLM-Review +1 AND Verified +1. Nothing is red; the \"ignore module-size -1\"\ncaveat no longer applies to anything. Top of stack is 1104 (a574).\n\nCLOSED THIS SESSION: 1484 (both seams: gate_dispatch 799->527, orchestrator 796->646), f844 (model\nclasses: schema, resolver, load_class_slots, CLI layer), a574 (typed region error + live bot fix,\nclass env_integration).\n\nPLAN-REVIEW STATE (verified with review-plan --status --ref HEAD, read-only):\n    cb6f certified | 172e certified | 7761 certified | cc33 certified | d23e unsigned (blocked)\nd23e is blocked by 172e AND 7761, so its review will fast-fail until both close.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION ORDER, and why: cc33 FIRST. The Bedrock experiment proved cc33 is a HARD PREREQUISITE\nfor eb6e — no single-scalar REBAR_LLM_MODEL reproduces today's 41-opus/1-sonnet split. Point it at\nBedrock sonnet and Pass-1 regresses opus->sonnet (violates the operator's \"Pass-1 opus-equivalent\"\nrequirement); point it at Bedrock opus and Pass-2/Pass-4 lose BOTH the cost downgrade and greedy\ndecoding. Then 172e and 7761 (both consume the same resolve_model_string), then d23e.\n\nTHE KEYSTONE, decided and recorded on eb58 (read BOTH comments — the first is superseded):\nClass names are RESERVED VALUES in the existing model-string space. `model: standard` and\n`model_ladder: [trivial, standard, frontier]`. NO new `class:` key, no schema change.\nThere is NO single funnel — my first claim of one was wrong three ways, all verified:\n  1. config.py cannot import model_classes at module level (dependency runs the other way)\n  2. model_ladder bypasses resolve_model (production_batch_runner.py:93 replaces cfg.model directly)\n  3. the verifier paths bypass it too (resolve_verifier_model, _verifier_cfg + xcheck.py:198/:336)\nShape is ONE FUNCTION, SEVERAL CALL SITES: model_classes.resolve_model_string(value). 172e wires the\nverifier sites; 7761 wires the ladder sites; config.resolve_model calls it via a LAZY function-level\nimport (precedent: config.py:108, :566, :768).\n\nMEASURED FACTS (do not re-derive):\n- Bedrock prompt caching WORKS on both sonnet and opus (cache_read 542,723 over a 19-call gate run).\n  Bug 7a79's silent no-op is a DIRECT-ANTHROPIC-path problem; Bedrock fixes it. Cost argument FOR.\n- Bedrock sonnet accepts temperature=0.0; Bedrock opus rejects every value except default 1.0. So\n  greedy Pass-2 survives on sonnet and is silently INOPERATIVE on opus. Keep the verifier on sonnet.\n- A real gate run on bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6 COMPLETED: 19 calls, substantive BLOCK\n  verdict, zero provider errors. The provider path is sound.\n- resolve_verifier_model (verify.py:269) downgrades ONLY on an exact match against the bare\n  \"claude-opus-4-8\". Even anthropic:claude-opus-4-8 — the SAME model, merely qualified — does not\n  downgrade. Verified by direct probe.\n- models_at_or_above(\"bedrock:...\") returns BARE Anthropic ids, so a Bedrock run escalates to DIRECT\n  ANTHROPIC on a context-limit error. Verified by direct probe. 7761 owns the fix.\n\ncc33's decisions are all cited to installed source (pydantic_ai/models/fallback.py, failure.py):\n  fallback_on accepts a CALLABLE predicate (fallback.py:92-107), so it reuses failure.py's EXISTING\n  _RETRYABLE frozenset (:51) plus CHANGE_PROVIDER_OR_MODEL — not a hand-maintained type tuple.\n  ran_model comes from ModelResponse.model_name (messages.py:2106); FallbackModel.model_name is a\n  synthetic \"fallback:a,b\" string, useless for attestation.\n  FallbackModel has no .profile and .provider returns None, so capabilities_for MUST NOT be called on\n  the wrapper — it would silently return DEFAULTS. Compute the conservative intersection over the\n  candidate list before wrapping.\n  Lifecycle is SPLIT: FallbackModel.__aenter__ enters sub-models via an AsyncExitStack, so pydantic-ai\n  closes ITS clients; rebar-created retrying transports stay on ProviderSession._closeables.\n\nSTILL OPEN: 343b gap 5 (provider_provenance never reaches a production payload; finalize_verdict\nunwired — now landable, 1484 freed 154 lines in orchestrator). eb58 (parent, in_progress, attestation\nstale-pin-drift by design — refresh ONCE at close time, after children settle). S6/S8/S9 blocked.\n\nOPERATOR ACTION OUTSTANDING: rotate ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. A subagent ran `env | grep -i anthropic` and\nprinted it into its transcript. That key being present is also what would let the provider-crossing\nescalation above succeed silently instead of failing loudly.\n\nPROCESS LESSONS THAT COST TIME THIS SESSION:\n- Backticks in a double-quoted zsh argument get command-substituted and SILENTLY STRIP content from\n  ticket bodies. Happened three times. Always write ticket bodies to a file and pass \"$(cat file)\",\n  or use subprocess with list args. ALWAYS probe the stored text afterwards; rc=0 proves nothing.\n- A material plan edit invalidates the prior PASS in SUBSTANCE. Two of f844's three later blocks were\n  caused by my own remediations. Re-review after every plan edit.\n- THREE staleness modes, same remedy, different triggers: stale-head (a landed commit moves HEAD),\n  stale-pin-drift (editing a CHILD invalidates the PARENT), stale-material (editing the ticket's own\n  plan). Only `review-plan --status --ref HEAD` surfaces them cheaply — it is free, use it first.\n- Structural constraints live OUTSIDE the behavioural test net. f844 passed 16/16 held-out tests with\n  six mutations caught and would STILL have failed CI on doc drift, because gen_env_registry.py only\n  sees LITERAL env-var strings. Put module size AND derived-file regeneration in the implementer brief.\n- The recurring failure mode across this epic: asserting an integration point exists without checking.\n  Reading the dependency's source FIRST (pydantic_ai/models/fallback.py, then failure.py) is what\n  finally converged cc33, after three review cycles that each caught an unverified claim."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785504895139739001,"uuid":"b0069ad7-48da-4e95-8581-8face8e6d319"}